WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.430 --> 00:00:04.760
ED GLAESER: If you grew up in an American or European city over the past quarter century,

00:00:04.760 --> 00:00:07.600
the odds are that your parents didn't work in a factory.

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:11.010
Factories today take up a lot of space, they use a lot of machinery and

00:00:11.010 --> 00:00:14.340
robotics, and they are typically located in a nice logistics hub

00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:16.080
somewhere far from the center of the city.

00:00:16.079 --> 00:00:19.640
But if you had grown up an American city a century ago,

00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:23.330
the odds were far better that someone in your family worked in a factory.

00:00:23.330 --> 00:00:26.920
My great grandfather worked in a steel mill near Pittsburgh.

00:00:26.920 --> 00:00:30.950
In this section we try to understand the link between cities and industry.

00:00:30.950 --> 00:00:34.790
One reason to care about the industrial city is to understand the post-industrial

00:00:34.790 --> 00:00:39.260
landscape of cities like Detroit, Cleveland, Manchester, and even New York.

00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:42.080
Many of these places where build up to enable manufacturing and

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:44.180
to house thousands of factory workers.

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:47.190
Today, those factories have left but the housing remains.

00:00:47.190 --> 00:00:51.380
And so many of the cities have decayed slowly but unceasingly.

00:00:51.380 --> 00:00:53.300
We hope for a better future for Detroit.

00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:56.660
But to get to that future we must start by understanding the past.

00:00:56.660 --> 00:00:59.550
A second reason why the tale of urban industrialization is so

00:00:59.550 --> 00:01:03.670
important is that it illuminates the connection between cities and creativity.

00:01:03.670 --> 00:01:06.830
Western cities will never live through another Industrial Revolution, but

00:01:06.830 --> 00:01:09.770
they may well experience some other technological watershed.

00:01:09.770 --> 00:01:12.260
They may well create such massive innovation.

00:01:12.260 --> 00:01:15.690
Perhaps if we understand the industrial past of cities better,

00:01:15.690 --> 00:01:18.715
we will be able to handle the next revolution with less pain.

00:01:18.715 --> 00:01:21.875
A final reason to think about the industrial city is that industrialization

00:01:21.875 --> 00:01:26.605
is still part of urbanization in many countries like China and Vietnam.

00:01:26.605 --> 00:01:29.575
I personally suspect that the Chinese cities will contain factories for

00:01:29.575 --> 00:01:31.990
only a few decades, and that pretty soon

00:01:31.990 --> 00:01:35.910
China will have to deal with places that look a lot like Detroit during the 1980s.

00:01:35.910 --> 00:01:37.770
But even if that prognosis is wrong,

00:01:37.770 --> 00:01:40.860
a better understanding of the link between cities and industrialization

00:01:40.860 --> 00:01:44.410
can improve policy making in the world's remaining manufacturing cities.

00:01:44.410 --> 00:01:46.850
Industry, with it's belching smoke stacks,

00:01:46.850 --> 00:01:49.310
was always an odd fit into the urban landscape.

00:01:49.310 --> 00:01:53.160
But it was, nonetheless, a critical part of our urban history.

